WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
Critical-thinking drills.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:08.189
This field runs on claims, like benchmark tables and we improved accuracy forty percent.

3
00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:11.033
Critical thinking here is a trainable skill.

4
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:14.667
Run this checklist before believing anything.

5
00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:16.000
Compared to what?

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.067
An untuned baseline means nothing.

7
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:19.443
Measured on what?

8
00:00:19.443 --> 00:00:21.533
Watch for test set contamination.

9
00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:22.714
Who's counting?

10
00:00:22.714 --> 00:00:26.000
Vendor evals of the vendor's product carry an incentive.

11
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.395
What's not shown?

12
00:00:27.395 --> 00:00:30.100
Cost, latency and failures usually vanish.

13
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.285
Does the mechanism make sense?

14
00:00:32.285 --> 00:00:33.667
Ask by what mechanism.

15
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.669
And would it survive my data?

16
00:00:35.669 --> 00:00:37.900
Run it on twenty of your own examples.

17
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.867
Now a weekly drill: red-team your own result.

18
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:45.900
After any positive result, spend ten minutes trying to prove it wrong.

19
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.233
Look for leakage, a tiny sample, a lucky seed, or judge bias.

20
00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:52.167
Publish only what survives.

21
00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:55.567
Another drill: predict before you run.

22
00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:58.090
Write down the expected number first.

23
00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.033
Being wrong on paper is how calibration is built.

24
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:03.600
Here it is in practice.

25
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.900
A launch post says accuracy improved forty percent.

26
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.067
You ask about the baseline, the dataset, who ran it, and what is missing.

27
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:14.933
Then you try twenty of your own examples before adopting anything.

28
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:18.833
The habit that makes it stick is a decision log.

29
00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:21.500
One line per decision: what you chose.

30
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.867
What you expected.

31
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.033
And later, what actually happened.

32
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:26.520
Review it monthly.

33
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.267
Ten years of unreviewed experience is often one year repeated ten times.

34
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:33.260
Let's recap.

35
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.700
Interrogate every claim: baseline, data, and source.

36
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.467
Red-team your own results.

37
00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.100
Predict before you run.

38
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.800
And keep a decision log.
